1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,640
Urine color - about 80 to 90 percent of bladder cancer cases start with red urine that doesn't hurt at all.

2
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:09,040
Here's what each urine color is actually telling you.

3
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:14,480
Urine color comes down to two things: a pigment called urochrome and how much water is in your system.

4
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:19,920
The more diluted your urine is, the lighter it looks - the more concentrated, the darker.

5
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:22,240
There are three color patterns worth knowing.

6
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:28,240
The yellow spectrum from pale to dark, pink or red urine, and dark brown or tea-colored urine.

7
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:31,200
First, pale straw yellow means you're well hydrated.

8
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:37,520
Dark yellow or amber means your body needs more fluids - that's the most common reason urine looks darker than usual.

9
00:00:37,840 --> 00:00:44,080
Second, pink or red urine usually points to a urinary tract infection, kidney stones, or an enlarged prostate.

10
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:53,040
Painless red urine deserves extra attention though - it's the most common first sign of bladder cancer, showing up in roughly 80 to 90 percent of cases before any other symptom appears.

11
00:00:53,360 --> 00:00:58,160
Third, dark brown or tea-colored urine can mean a liver or bile duct problem.

12
00:00:58,480 --> 00:01:07,600
It can also show up after an extremely intense workout, when muscle breakdown called rhabdomyolysis releases myoglobin into your bloodstream - that combination with severe muscle pain needs emergency care.

13
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:10,960
A little extra darkness usually just means you need more water.

14
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:18,320
Painless red urine is the one to take seriously and get checked, and tea-colored urine along with severe muscle pain needs urgent care too.
